---

<DocsLayout slug="tasks">
  <DocsVisual
    label="The Tasks view with six tasks grouped under Ready for review, In progress and Backlog. Each row shows its status, a title, the project and when the task last changed. The two tasks ready for review show their label and a Review button, and the backlog tasks show a comment mark."
  >
    <TaskListMock />
  </DocsVisual>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-tasks-work">
    <h2 id="how-tasks-work">How tasks work</h2>
    <p>A task is a piece of work written down before anyone builds it. It has a title, a spec in Markdown, a status and a priority, and it belongs to one project.</p>
    <p>Saving a task starts nothing: no agent runs and no worktree is created. When you are ready, the work happens in a thread, the task's execution thread, where you follow it like any other conversation.</p>
    <p>Use a task to plan work you want to read and edit before an agent builds it, or to keep a backlog. An agent can also save a task for you when you want work noted for later instead of done now.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="create">
    <h2 id="create">Create a task</h2>
    <h3>From the Tasks view</h3>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>Tasks</strong> in the sidebar and choose <strong>New task</strong>, or press <kbd>⌘⇧N</kbd> from anywhere.</li>
      <li>Write a title. Under it, pick a priority and the project.</li>
      <li>Write the spec below.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Create task</strong>, or press <kbd>⌘↵</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The task is saved to <strong>Backlog</strong> and opens on its Overview. While you write, the draft is kept on your computer, so leaving the screen loses nothing.</p>
    <h3>From a thread</h3>
    <p>Choose <strong>Tasks</strong> in the thread's header, then <strong>Add</strong>. The new task is linked to the thread, belongs to its project and uses its location.</p>
    <h3>By an agent</h3>
    <p>Agents in a thread have task tools. Ask one to "add a task for the login redirect" and it saves the task with <code>task_create</code>. The task appears as a card in the transcript and under the thread's <strong>Tasks</strong>. Agents can also list, read and update tasks in the project. If the thread already has a task with the same title, or with mostly the same words, OpenOrc returns that task instead of saving a second one.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="spec">
    <h2 id="spec">Write the spec</h2>
    <p>The spec lives on the task's <strong>Overview</strong> tab, in an editor that saves Markdown.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Type <kbd>/</kbd> to insert a heading, a list, a checklist, a quote, a code block, a divider or an image.</li>
      <li>Select text to make it bold or italic, or to add a link.</li>
      <li>Paste or drop images into the spec.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Markdown</strong> to edit the source, and <strong>Rich text</strong> to go back.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Changes save shortly after you stop typing, and <kbd>⌘S</kbd> saves at once. The footer says <strong>Saved</strong> when the task is up to date. Below the spec, <strong>Labels</strong> takes a comma-separated list. Further down are the task's comments (see <a href={docHref("discussions")}>Task discussions</a>), its <strong>Execution settings</strong>, <strong>Activity</strong>, which lists the task's threads and runs, and <strong>Delete task</strong>. Deleting a task removes it and its discussion; its thread keeps its messages and changes.</p>
    <h3 id="refine">Refine with an agent</h3>
    <p><strong>Refine</strong>, beside <strong>Markdown</strong> above the spec, asks an agent to improve the task itself.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>Refine</strong>. Say what should change, or leave the field empty, and choose the model or Orcling under <strong>Refine with</strong>. Then click <strong>Refine</strong>.</li>
      <li>The agent reads the project and the task's comments without changing anything, and hands back a suggested title, spec, and labels. The suggestion shows in place of the spec, with its changes marked.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Use suggestion</strong> to change the task. <strong>Edit</strong> opens the suggestion for your corrections first, <strong>Refine again</strong> asks for another try, and <strong>Dismiss</strong> leaves the task as it was.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The task changes only when you use the suggestion. Tasks in OpenOrc Cloud don't offer Refine.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="status">
    <h2 id="status">Status and priority</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Backlog</strong></td>
            <td>Saved and not started. Every new task starts here.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>In progress</strong></td>
            <td>Work on it has started.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Review</strong></td>
            <td>The work is ready for you to check. These tasks lead the list, and <strong>Tasks</strong> in the sidebar counts them.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Done</strong></td>
            <td>Finished.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Archived</strong></td>
            <td>Put away. Agents no longer see it when they list tasks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Proposed</strong></td>
            <td>Waiting for your go-ahead.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Change a status from the status menu on the Overview, or click a task's status icon in the Tasks list. When an agent starts the work, the task moves to <strong>In progress</strong>, and the agent records later progress with its task tools, so a task can reach <strong>Review</strong> or <strong>Done</strong> without you. You can always set the status yourself.</p>
    <p>On a project's <a href={docHref("board")}>board</a>, a status you set moves the task's card to the first column of that stage, and that column's agent starts.</p>
    <p>Priority is one of <strong>No priority</strong>, <strong>Low</strong>, <strong>Medium</strong>, <strong>High</strong> or <strong>Urgent</strong>. It shows beside the status at the top of the task's Overview.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tabs">
    <h2 id="tabs">The task's tabs</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tab</th>
            <th scope="col">What it shows</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Overview</strong></td>
            <td>The spec, the task's properties, comments, execution settings and history.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Activity</strong></td>
            <td>The task's history: a line for each move, each agent a column started and each update an agent posted. Work done in the task's thread stays in that thread. See <a href={docHref("task-history")}>Task history and updates</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Changes</strong></td>
            <td>The changes in the task's thread, in the panel beside the task. See <a href={docHref("review")}>Changes and review</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Commits</strong></td>
            <td>The commits made in the task's thread, with the controls to push and open a pull request.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Memory</strong></td>
            <td>The project's memory, beside the task. See <a href={docHref("memory")}>Project memory</a>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Changes, Commits and Memory show the panel of the task's thread, so they stay empty until the task has one. Changes and Commits say <strong>No execution yet</strong> until then.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="start">
    <h2 id="start">Start a task</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Choose <strong>Open thread</strong> at the bottom of the task's Overview. In the Tasks list, a backlog task's row has the same button as a message icon.</li>
      <li>OpenOrc opens the task's thread. If the task has none yet, it reuses the thread the task came from when that thread is idle and works in the same place. Otherwise it creates a thread named after the task, with the task already written in the message box.</li>
      <li>Check the model in the message box, add any direction, and send. Opening a thread starts nothing; the agent starts when you send.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The work runs in that thread's folder: your checkout, or a worktree of the thread's own. When OpenOrc creates a thread for the task, the task's <strong>Execution location</strong> decides where it works: <strong>Local checkout</strong> or <strong>Worktree</strong>, under <strong>Execution settings</strong> on the Overview. A new task takes the location of the thread it came from, or else your <strong>New thread workspace</strong> setting. Once the thread has started, changing the location moves the thread and its uncommitted changes. See <a href={docHref("git")}>Git and worktrees</a>.</p>
    <p>You can also start the work from the task's comments, by asking a model to build it. See <a href={docHref("discussions") + "#work"}>From discussion to work</a>.</p>
    <Callout>Once a task has a thread, its work stays there. An agent in another thread can't start it, so continue in the task's thread, and move that thread if the work belongs somewhere else.</Callout>
    <h3 id="model">The task's model</h3>
    <p>Under <strong>Execution settings</strong>, below <strong>Execution location</strong>, <strong>Model</strong> chooses the model the task's work runs on. For a Claude model, a menu for the account appears beside it.</p>
    <p>Agents started for the task use it: its first thread, the agent of a <a href={docHref("board") + "#agents"}>board column</a>, the agent an <a href={docHref("automations")}>automation</a> starts, and a task button that makes its own thread. A column, an automation or a button that names its own model uses that one instead.</p>
    <p>The choice applies to threads made from then on. The task's existing thread keeps its model. Left at <strong>Your usual model</strong>, the first choice, those agents follow the task's thread, or your default when the task has no thread yet.</p>
    <h3 id="threads">Several threads for one task</h3>
    <p>A task can have more than one thread. They are listed under <strong>Activity</strong> on the Overview, the current one first. <strong>Continue in a new thread</strong> there starts a fresh session in the task's folder, from the task and a brief of the last thread. It uses the current thread's model and account unless you choose others. <strong>Open thread</strong> then goes to the new thread, and the earlier one stays listed.</p>
    <p>Before a task has a thread, the arrow beside <strong>Open thread</strong> chooses the first thread's model and account.</p>
    <h3 id="buttons">Task buttons</h3>
    <p>A task button sends a prompt you saved, with the task. A project's buttons show beside <strong>Open thread</strong> on its tasks. To add one, open the project's <strong>Project settings</strong>, find <strong>Task buttons</strong>, and click <strong>Add button</strong>.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Setting</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Label</strong></td>
            <td>The button's name, such as <strong>Review</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Show on</strong></td>
            <td>The task statuses the button appears on. Archived tasks show no buttons.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Prompt</strong></td>
            <td>What the button sends. Type <kbd>/</kbd> to call one of the agent's skills.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Include the task</strong></td>
            <td>On, the task goes after the prompt. Off, the prompt goes alone.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Send right away</strong></td>
            <td>Off, the button opens its thread with the message waiting in the message box instead of sending it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Runs in</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Current thread</strong> sends in the task's own thread. <strong>New thread</strong> starts a thread of the button's own, listed under the task, that works in the task's folder.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mode</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Default</strong> uses the task thread's mode. Or choose one of the agent's <a href={docHref("permissions")}>modes</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Model and account</td>
            <td><strong>Default</strong> uses the task thread's. Or choose others for the button.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A button sent in the task's thread moves a task that hasn't started to <strong>In progress</strong>, and leaves one in <strong>Review</strong> or <strong>Done</strong> where it is.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="list">
    <h2 id="list">The Tasks list</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Active</strong>, <strong>Done</strong> and <strong>Archived</strong> split the list. Active holds every task that is neither done nor archived.</li>
      <li>On Active, the status menu narrows the list to one status. Each option shows how many tasks it has.</li>
      <li><strong>Filter tasks…</strong> matches titles and labels.</li>
      <li>The project chip in the header switches between one project and <strong>All projects</strong>. With all projects, each row names its project.</li>
      <li>Tasks are grouped by status. Click a group's heading to fold it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The list works from the keyboard: <kbd>j</kbd> and <kbd>k</kbd>, or the arrow keys, move between tasks, and <kbd>↵</kbd> opens one.</p>
    <h3 id="board">The Board</h3>
    <p>The switch in the header chooses <strong>List</strong> or <strong>Board</strong>. The Board shows the same tasks as cards in columns, which you drag to move. A project's board has columns of its own, and a column can start an agent for each card that arrives. See <a href={docHref("board")}>Task board</a>.</p>
    <h3>Tasks in OpenOrc Cloud</h3>
    <p>
      In an <a href={docHref("organizations")}>organization</a>, the Tasks list holds the team's tasks, which carry the cloud mark. A task can be assigned to a person, and <strong>Assigned to me</strong> narrows the list to yours. In
      <strong>My workspaces</strong>, the list also shows the tasks your agents made in your cloud threads, with the cloud mark, beside this computer's own. Only you see them. <strong>New task</strong> in My workspaces still makes a task on this
      computer.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a team task, <strong>Open thread</strong> asks where its work runs: <strong>On my computer</strong>, in a thread in the project's folder on this Mac, or <strong>On OpenOrc Cloud</strong>. The task then shows where it runs, such as
      <strong>On Dana's computer</strong>, and nobody opens a second thread for it. Deleting the thread on your computer frees the task again.
    </p>
    <p>
      An agent working on a team task, on someone's computer or on OpenOrc Cloud, posts its updates on the task, and everyone who reads the task sees them in its activity, with whose agent wrote each and where it ran. When an update offers
      replies, the task's assignee answers (anyone can while it has no assignee), and the answer reaches the agent's thread wherever it runs. An agent on a computer that is off gets it once that computer is back.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "board" }, { slug: "task-history" }, { slug: "discussions" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
